  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Saturday)\nIssued at 826 AM CDT SUN JUL 26 2026\n\nCentral Alabama remains in an unsettled pattern through the\nweekend as the stalled cold front lifts north. We are currently\nmonitoring a weak impulse moving through, accompanied by a\nboundary shifting into our northern counties. Precipitation\ncoverage will be highest in the southeast this morning, with\nadditional boundary-driven activity expected later today as the\nnorthern feature moves south. As we work into the afternoon, the\nboundary in our north will be across the south, while an\nadditional boundary may begin to slide into the north from\nactivity currently in Kentucky. These boundaries will trigger\nmore showers and storms. Activity in the north will be limited as\nthe bulk of the instability will be in the central and southern\nsections of the area. Overall coverage will be isolated to\nscattered, so generally only have 20-40% rain chances in the\nafternoon to account for this, with the highest in the central\nand south. Showers and storms may linger through 9 or 10 PM in\nthe far south, before dissipating for the night. Cloud cover and\nrain should keep the temperatures down in the southeast through\nthe early afternoon as well. In the north and west, we will see\ntemperatures in the low to mid 90s, so afternoon heat index\nvalues will climb above 100 and approach the 105 Heat Advisory\ncriteria. While heat index values in the north and west will\napproach the 105\u00c2\u00b0F threshold, current dewpoints are trending\nlower than in previous days. Consequently, confidence is too low\nfor a Heat Advisory, though isolated areas may briefly touch\n105\u00c2\u00b0F this afternoon.\n\nWhile the ridge building will lead to rising temperatures\nbeginning Monday, a mid-level shortwave could introduce a\nMesoscale Convective System (MCS) from the north; while guidance\nsignal strength for this event has increased, confidence in its\nsize and intensity remains low. Consequently, only low rain\nchances remain on Monday. Subject to model consistency, increased\nprecipitation chances are possible Monday, mainly north of I-20.\n\nTuesday is shaping up to be the hottest day of the week, with\nhighs reaching the mid to upper-90s under the influence of the\nridge. By mid-week, the ridge appears to become slightly\nsuppressed as a trough builds over the East Coast and a deep low\nforms over New England. This pattern change will allow for\nslightly cooler temperatures to filter in from the north, with\nhighs trending toward the upper-80s and low-90s by next Friday.\nConfidence in the timing and intensity of potential MCS systems\nremains limited, resulting in lower probabilities of\nprecipitation for the latter half of the workweek.\n\n16",
